:title("Bash to LLVM IR compiler") ;
:description("Compiles the same practical Bash that bash-interpreter.abnf interprets to an LLVM IR
module, then runs it with the built-in IR interpreter. Where the interpreter walks the tree and
executes it, this grammar composes a code generator ('thunk') per tag and emits real IR: control
flow (if/elif/else, while, until, for, C-style for, case, pipelines, subshells, && / ||, break,
continue, functions and return) becomes basic blocks and branches, and every Bash value is an
i8* into a runtime string world. break and continue are ordinary branches to the loop's own
blocks - the loop being emitted knows both targets - so no control signal travels at run time.

Bash has one data type, the string, so the emitted module carries a string runtime hand-built as
IR functions: rt_bump bump-allocates from a global arena, rt_strlen / rt_streq / rt_strcmp /
rt_strcat / rt_substr operate on NUL-terminated i8*, rt_int2str / rt_str2int convert to and from
i32, rt_glob / rt_class are the glob matcher behind case and [[ == ]], rt_strip / rt_replace /
rt_case implement the ${v#p} ${v/p/r} ${v^^} family, rt_putc / rt_cap_begin / rt_cap_end give
command substitution and pipelines their output capture, rt_read_line / rt_field / rt_splitifs
back read and word splitting, rt_arr_* are the array store, and rt_fskind models the handful of
standard paths the file predicates ask about.

Three representation choices carry most of the semantics. Variables live in ONE global array of
i8* indexed by a compile-time slot id, which is what makes `local` work: rt_push_local records a
slot's previous value on a save stack, a function snapshots the stack depth on entry, and every
ret unwinds back to it. Positional parameters live in a second global stack with a base and a
count, so \"$@\" can expand to any number of words and shift and set -- are ordinary writes; a
call pushes its field-expanded arguments and restores base, count and top afterwards, which is
what makes recursion nest. And a word whose expansion can yield several fields is carried as an
encoded FIELD LIST (a leading 0x02, each field followed by 0x01), so \"$@\", \"${a[@]}\" and an
unquoted expansion split on IFS all flow through the same one representation; a builtin with a
fixed argument list flattens it with rt_wordjoin.

A subshell snapshots the whole variable array (rt_ss_save) plus the shell options, and redirects
the emitter's exit target so an `exit` inside it ends only the subshell. A variable that was
never assigned holds a distinct empty string, the unset marker, which is how ${v-default} tells
unset from empty and how set -u recognises an unbound read. eval and `trap ... EXIT` re-parse
their argument with THIS grammar and emit it inline; a computed eval is lowered only for the
assignment form NAME=VALUE, through a runtime name-to-slot dispatch.

All 22 sections of tests/bash-test-full.sh pass, goja and -frozen byte-identical.

[[ str =~ ere ]] is the one construct that needs help from outside the emitted module: it calls
rt_regex_search / rt_regex_error, two externs the IR machine provides, backed by the SAME
rxCompile/rxSearch engine the interpreter grammar reaches through lib/regex.js - there is no
second matcher, so the two halves cannot drift. rt_regex_search answers the capture-PAIR count
(exactly ${#BASH_REMATCH[@]}), 0 for no match, -1 for a pattern that did not compile and -2 for
the engine's step cap; the caps array it fills carries BYTE offsets into the subject, which is
what bash indexes, and a group that did not participate reads -1/-1 and becomes bash's
empty-but-counted BASH_REMATCH entry.

IMPORTANT for -exe: rt_regex_search has NO C symbol, so a native build has nothing to link it
against. The link still succeeds, because stubUndefined gives every declared-but-undefined
non-libc function a zero-returning body - and since 0 means NO MATCH, a =~ in a native binary
degrades to 'never matches' rather than to a silent false match. That is deliberate but it is
still wrong, so a native build that uses =~ needs a real implementation of rt_regex_search
linked in. Measured: the whole ratchet file compiled with -exe and run natively passes 252 of
its 259 checks, and the 7 that fail are exactly the =~ assertions of section 11.

The generated module is printed (this is the compiler output); it is valid LLVM IR and can be
handed to clang. Passing -exe PATH to mec links it into a native executable with clang directly
(mec languages/bash-to-llvm-ir.abnf script.sh -exe out && ./out). The supported language, and
what is modelled rather than real (no filesystem, no environment, no process id, stderr as a
channel whose text is dropped), is the one documented in bash-interpreter.abnf. In addition,
this compiler requires the command word of a simple command to be a literal (static dispatch),
and the arrays a subshell ADDS are not rolled back when it ends.") ;


// --- main rules

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// A whole script: statements separated by ; or newlines (blank / comment lines collapse).
// The production tag runs all top level statement thunks and exits with the final status.
Program     <~~ runProgram(takeAll()) ~~>
            = OptSep [ Statement { Sep Statement } OptSep ] ;

// Statement separators: one or more ; or newlines. Leading whitespace (incl. #comments) is
// skipped before each separator token, so blank and comment-only lines vanish here.
OptSep      = [ Sep ] ;
Sep         = SepTok { SepTok } ;
SepTok      = ";" | "\n" ;

Statement   = AndOrList ;

// A brace / keyword delimited body: at least one statement. The trailing OptSep swallows the
// final ; or newline before the closing keyword (fi / done / }), and a reserved word in
// command position (guarded in SimpleCommand) stops the list so that keyword can match.
CompoundList <~~ push(makeSeq(takeAll())) ~~>
            = OptSep Statement { Sep Statement } OptSep ;


// --- compound statements

// if LIST; then LIST; [elif LIST; then LIST;]* [else LIST;] fi
IfStmt      <~~ push(makeIf(takeAll())) ~~>
            = IfClause { ElifClause } [ ElseClause ] KwFi ;
IfClause    <~~ push(mkCond(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwIf CompoundList KwThen CompoundList ;
ElifClause  <~~ push(mkCond(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwElif CompoundList KwThen CompoundList ;
ElseClause  <~~ push(mkElse(pop())) ~~>
            = KwElse CompoundList ;

// case WORD in [(] PAT [| PAT]... ) LIST ;; ... esac
// The body of a clause is an ordinary statement list, except that its separator run must not
// swallow the leading ";" of the ";;" that ends the clause - which is what NotCaseTerm guards.
CaseStmt    <~~ push(makeCase(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwCase Word KwIn CaseSepOpt { CaseClause } KwEsac ;
CaseClause  <~~ push(mkCaseClause(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ "(" ] CasePats ")" CaseBody [ CaseTerm ] CaseSepOpt ;
CasePats    <~~ push({pats: takeAll()}) ~~> = Word { "|" Word } ;
CaseBody    <~~ push(makeSeq(takeAll())) ~~>
            = CaseSepOpt [ Statement { CaseSep Statement } CaseSepOpt ] ;
CaseSep     = NotCaseTerm SepTok { NotCaseTerm SepTok } ;
CaseSepOpt  = [ CaseSep ] ;
CaseTerm    = CaseNl CaseTermTok ;
CaseNl      = { "\n" } ;
CaseTermTok <~~ push({cterm: up.in}) ~~> = ";;&" | ";;" | ";&" ;

// while LIST; do LIST; done   /   until LIST; do LIST; done (the negated condition)
WhileStmt   <~~ push(makeWhile(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwWhile CompoundList KwDo CompoundList KwDone ;
UntilStmt   <~~ push(makeUntil(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwUntil CompoundList KwDo CompoundList KwDone ;

// for (( init; cond; step )); do LIST; done
CForStmt    <~~ push(makeCFor(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwFor "((" :whitespace(Whitespace) ArList ";" ArList ";" ArList "))" OptSep KwDo CompoundList KwDone ;

// for NAME in WORD...; do LIST; done  -- each word is one iteration value (no re-splitting).
// for NAME in WORD...; do LIST; done   /   for NAME; do LIST; done (the positional parameters)
ForStmt     <~~ push(makeFor(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwFor ForName [ ForIn ] Sep KwDo CompoundList KwDone ;
ForIn       = KwIn ForWords ;
ForName     <~~ push({fname: up.in}) ~~>
            = Name ;
ForWords    <~~ push({words: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = { CWord } ;

// NAME() { LIST; }, and the `function NAME [()]  { LIST; }` keyword form.
FuncDef     <~~ push(makeFuncDef(takeAll())) ~~>
            = FuncDefHead OptSep "{" CompoundList "}" ;
FuncDefHead = ( KwFunction FuncName [ "(" ")" ] ) | ( FuncName "(" ")" ) ;
FuncName    <~~ push({fname: up.in}) ~~>
            = Name ;

Name        = ( "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "_" ) :whitespace() { "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "0"..."9" | "_" } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;


// --- simple commands, && / || lists and assignments

// cmd1 && cmd2 || cmd3 : left to right, short circuit on the running exit status.
AndOrOp     <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~>
            = "&&" | "||" ;

// A pipeline is what && / || and ! actually operate on. Each stage runs with its stdout
// captured and handed to the next stage as its standard input.
AndOrList   <~~ push(makeAndOr(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Pipeline { AndOrOp Pipeline } ;
Pipeline    <~~ push(makePipeline(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ BangOp ] Command { PipeOp Command } ;
BangOp      <~~ push({bang: 1}) ~~> = "!" KwEnd ;
PipeOp      = "|" NotBar ;

// A compound command is an ordinary Command, so it can be a pipeline stage
// (`echo x | while read l; do ...; done`) and can carry redirections that apply to the
// whole thing (`while read l; do ...; done < file`, `for x in ...; done <<EOF`).
Command     = CompoundRedir | Subshell | BraceGroup | LetCmd | DeclCommand | PrefixCommand | Assignment | SimpleCommand ;
CompoundRedir <~~ push(mkRedirected(takeAll())) ~~> = CompoundCore [ RedirList ] ;
// "((" must be tried before "(" or an arithmetic command parses as nested subshells, which
// is why CompoundRedir comes before Subshell rather than after it.
CompoundCore = ArithCmd | CondCmd | IfStmt | CForStmt | ForStmt | WhileStmt | UntilStmt | CaseStmt | FuncDef ;

// NAME=VALUE... COMMAND ARGS : the assignments apply only for the duration of the command.
// Tried before a bare Assignment, which is the same shape without a command word after it.
PrefixCommand <~~ push(makePrefixCmd(takeAll())) ~~>
            = AsgPrefix { AsgPrefix } NotReserved CWord { CmdSuffix } ;
AsgPrefix   <~~ push(mkPrefixAsg(takeAll())) ~~>
            = AsgIdent <~~ push({aname: up.in}) ~~> :whitespace() AsgEq :whitespace() [ Word ] ;
AsgEq       = "=" NotEq ;

// ( LIST ) runs in a copy of the shell: variable changes and `exit` stay inside.
Subshell    <~~ push(makeSubshell(pop())) ~~> = "(" CompoundList ")" [ RedirList ] ;
// { LIST; } is a plain grouping: it runs in the CURRENT shell.
BraceGroup  <~~ push(makeGroup(pop())) ~~> = "{" CompoundList "}" [ RedirList ] ;
// (( expr )) as a command: status 0 when the value is non-zero.
ArithCmd    <~~ push(makeArithCmd(pop())) ~~> = "((" :whitespace(Whitespace) ArList "))" ;
// [[ ... ]] : the conditional command (no word splitting, glob and regex matching).
CondCmd     <~~ push(makeCondCmd(pop())) ~~> = "[[" :whitespace(Whitespace) CondOr "]]" ;

RedirList   = RedirItem { RedirItem } ;
RedirItem   = HereDoc | Redir ;

// let EXPR ... : the same evaluator as (( )); a quoted argument is parsed as arithmetic.
LetCmd      <~~ push(makeLetCmd(takeAll())) ~~> = KwLet LetArg { LetArg } ;
KwLet       = "let" KwEnd ;
LetArg      = LetDq | LetSq | ArAssign ;
LetDq       = '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ArList '"' ;
LetSq       = "'" :whitespace(Whitespace) ArList "'" ;

// local / declare / typeset / export / readonly [-flags] NAME[=WORD] ...
// Spelled as its own production (rather than an ordinary command with word arguments)
// because the declared NAME has to be known statically: the compiler needs it to pick a
// variable slot, and `local` needs it to record the save entry for the current call frame.
DeclCommand <~~ push(makeDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = DeclKw { DeclFlag } { DeclItem } ;
DeclKw      <~~ push({dkw: up.in}) ~~> = DeclWord KwEnd ;
DeclWord    = "local" | "declare" | "typeset" | "export" | "readonly" ;
DeclFlag    <~~ push({dflag: up.in}) ~~> = "-" :whitespace() DeclFlagCh { DeclFlagCh } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
DeclFlagCh  = "a" | "A" | "i" | "r" | "x" | "u" | "l" | "g" | "n" | "p" | "f" | "F" ;
DeclItem    = DeclAssign | DeclName ;
DeclAssign  <~~ push(mkDeclItem(takeAll())) ~~>
            = AsgIdent <~~ push({aname: up.in}) ~~> AsgTail ;
DeclName    <~~ push({dname: up.in}) ~~> = Name ;

// name=value : the = must stick to the name (no surrounding spaces), so the disable
// (:whitespace()) sits in THIS sequence, right after the identifier. The value is optional
// (name= is valid) and starts immediately after = with no space.
Assignment  <~~ push(makeAssign(takeAll())) ~~>
            = AsgIdent <~~ push({aname: up.in}) ~~> AsgTail ;

// The right hand side of an assignment: a word, or a parenthesised array literal. `+=`
// appends (a string for a scalar, elements for an array).
AsgTail     = [ AsgIdx ] :whitespace() AsgOp2 :whitespace() AsgRhs ;
AsgIdx      <~~ push({aidx: 1}) ~~> = "[" IdxWord "]" ;
AsgOp2      <~~ push({aop2: up.in}) ~~> = "+=" | AsgEq ;
AsgRhs      = ArrayLit | AsgWord ;
AsgWord     = [ Word ] ;
ArrayLit    <~~ push(mkArrayLit(takeAll())) ~~> = "(" :whitespace(WsNL) ArrayItems ")" ;
ArrayItems  = { ArrayItem } ;
ArrayItem   = ArrayKV | CWord ;
ArrayKV     <~~ push(mkArrayKV(takeAll())) ~~> = "[" IdxWord "]" :whitespace() "=" :whitespace() Word ;
WsNL        = { @+" \t\r\n" | Comment } ;
AsgIdent    = ( "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "_" ) :whitespace() { "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "0"..."9" | "_" } ;

// A command word followed by argument words. NotReserved blocks a bare reserved keyword in
// command position (so 'done' / 'fi' / ... end an enclosing list instead of being commands).
SimpleCommand <~~ push(makeSimpleCommand(takeAll())) ~~>
            = NotReserved CWord { CmdSuffix } ;
CmdSuffix   = HereDoc | Redir | CWord ;

// A here-document. Its body lives on the FOLLOWING lines, so it cannot be described by
// ordinary productions: HdScan is a lexer that scans from the marker to the terminator line
// and returns ONE token covering all of it (minus the terminator's own newline, which stays
// behind as the statement separator), and mkHereDoc splits that text apart again.
//
// LIMIT: the marker must be the last thing on its line. Bash also allows code after it
// (`cat <<EOF; echo --`, `cat <<A 3<<B`), which would need the body to be consumed where the
// LINE ends rather than at the marker; HdScan refuses those rather than mis-parsing them, so
// they stay an honest parse failure - as does <<< , which RedirTok handles instead.
HereDoc     <~~ push(mkHereDoc(up.in)) ~~> = HdScan :whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// The body of an unquoted here-document, re-parsed with this grammar. mec -verify reports
// HereBodyW and its parts as unreachable, which is correct and expected: nothing in the start
// rule refers to them, they are entered through c.parseFrom from mkHereDoc.
// Literal runs, escapes
// and $ expansions, exactly like the inside of a double-quoted string. The leading "\x01" is
// prepended by mkHereDoc so the production can start with a token and then turn whitespace
// skipping off (a production may not START with a Command - that is read as a repetition).
HereBodyW   <~~ push(mkWord(takeAll())) ~~> = "\x01" :whitespace() HereSegs ;
HereSegs    = { HereSeg } ;
HereSeg     = ExpSeg | DqEsc | HereLit ;
HereLit     <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = !@+"$\\`" ;

// Redirections. `<` declines a second "<" so a here-document HdScan refused cannot fall
// through and be mis-read as two input redirections.
Redir       <~~ push(mkRedir(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ IoNum ] RedirOp [ Word ] ;
IoNum       <~~ push({iofd: up.in}) ~~> = "0"..."9" :whitespace() { "0"..."9" } ;
RedirOp     <~~ push({redir: up.in}) ~~> = RedirTok ;
RedirTok    = "<<<" | "&>>" | "&>" | ">>" | ">&" | ">|" | "<&" | ">" | LtOne ;
LtOne       = "<" NotLt ;


// --- words: a run of segments with NO whitespace between them ---------------------
// The first segment is matched under the caller's whitespace state (so leading space is
// skipped in argument position, but NOT after = in an assignment); :whitespace() then turns
// skipping off for the remaining segments so foo\"bar\"$x concatenates as one word.

Word        <~~ push(mkWord(takeAll())) ~~>
            = WordFirst :whitespace() { WordSeg } ;
WordFirst   = Tilde | WordSeg ;
WordSeg     = SqSeg | DqSeg | AnsiSeg | UqExp | LineCont | EscSeg | BraceLit | BareSeg ;

// An expansion OUTSIDE quotes is subject to word splitting on IFS; the same production
// inside a double-quoted string (or a ${...} word) is not, which is the only difference
// between the two - so the split-able form is marked here rather than in the expansion.
UqExp       <~~ push(mkSplittable(pop())) ~~> = ExpSeg ;

// A word in COMMAND ARGUMENT position, where brace expansion applies and one written word
// can therefore become several. Real bash does not brace-expand an assignment's right hand
// side (a=x{1,2} really is the four characters x{1,2}), which is why this is its own rule.
CWord       <~~ pushWords(mkWords(takeAll())) ~~>
            = CWordFirst :whitespace() { CWordSeg } ;
CWordFirst  = Tilde | CWordSeg ;
CWordSeg    = SqSeg | DqSeg | AnsiSeg | UqExp | LineCont | EscSeg | BraceRange | BraceAlts | BraceLit | BareSeg ;

// {a,b,c} needs at least one comma; {x} is literal. {1..5} {5..1} {a..e} {1..9..3} are ranges.
BraceAlts   <~~ push(mkBraceAlts(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" :whitespace() BraceAlt "," BraceAlt { "," BraceAlt } "}" ;
BraceAlt    <~~ push({alt: takeAll()}) ~~> = { BraceAltSeg } ;
BraceAltSeg = SqSeg | DqSeg | AnsiSeg | ExpSeg | EscSeg | BraceRange | BraceAlts | AltBare ;
AltBare     <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = !@+" \t\n\r;#$'\"\\(){},&|<>`" ;
BraceRange  <~~ push(mkBraceRange(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" :whitespace() RangeEnd ".." RangeEnd [ RangeStep ] "}" ;
RangeEnd    <~~ push({rend: up.in}) ~~> = RangeTok ;
RangeStep   <~~ push({rstep: up.in}) ~~> = ".." RangeNum ;
RangeTok    = RangeNum | RangeAlpha ;
RangeNum    = [ "-" ] "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" } ;
RangeAlpha  = "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" ;
BraceLit    <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = "{" | "}" ;

// ~ or ~/... at the START of a word expands to $HOME.
Tilde       <~~ push(mkTilde()) ~~> = "~" TildeEnd ;

// A backslash immediately followed by a newline is a line continuation: both vanish.
LineCont    <~~ push(makeConst("")) ~~> = "\\" "\n" ;

// $'...' : ANSI-C quoting. The body is taken raw and decoded by ansiC (\n \t \xHH \0NNN ...).
AnsiSeg     = "$'" :whitespace() AnsiBody "'" ;
AnsiBody    <~~ push(makeConst(ansiC(up.in))) ~~> = { AnsiEsc | AnsiLit } ;
AnsiEsc     = "\\" AnyCh ;
AnsiLit     = !@+"'\\" ;

// 'single quoted' : everything up to the next ' is literal (no escapes, may span lines).
SqSeg       = "'" :whitespace() { !@+"'" } <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> "'" ;

// "double quoted" : literal runs, escapes and $ expansions concatenated.
DqSeg       <~~ push(mkDqWord(takeAll())) ~~>
            = '"' :whitespace() { ExpSeg | DqCont | DqEsc | DqLit } '"' ;
DqLit       <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~>
            = !@+"\"$\\`" ;
DqEsc       <~~ push(makeConst(dqEsc(up.in))) ~~>
            = "\\" AnyCh ;
DqCont      <~~ push(makeConst("")) ~~> = "\\" "\n" ;

// An unquoted \x escape: the backslash is dropped, the next char is literal (\  -> space).
EscSeg      <~~ push(makeConst(escChar(up.in))) ~~>
            = "\\" AnyCh ;

// A bare run: any char that is not whitespace, a separator, or a shell metacharacter.
BareSeg     <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~>
            = !@+" \t\n\r;#$'\"\\(){}&|<>`" ;

AnyCh       = "\x00"..."\x09" | "\x0b"..."\U0010ffff" ;


// --- $ expansions -----------------------------------------------------------------

ExpSeg      = ArithExp | CmdSub | BackSub | DollarBrace | DollarName | DollarDigit | DollarSpecial | DollarLit ;

// $( LIST ) and the older `LIST` : run the list with stdout captured, and the value of the
// word segment is that output with its trailing newlines removed (exactly like the shell).
// The list is parsed with the normal whitespace rule; the enclosing word's "no skipping"
// state is restored automatically when the production returns.
CmdSub      <~~ push(mkCmdSub(pop())) ~~>
            = "$(" :whitespace(Whitespace) CompoundList ")" ;
BackSub     <~~ push(mkCmdSub(pop())) ~~>
            = "`" :whitespace(Whitespace) CompoundList "`" ;

// $(( expr )) : whitespace IS allowed inside; the value is the decimal string of the result.
ArithExp    <~~ push(mkArith(pop())) ~~>
            = "$((" :whitespace(Whitespace) ArList "))" ;

// ${...} : the whole parameter-expansion family. Inside the braces whitespace is NOT
// skipped (a default word may contain spaces), and the enclosing state is restored on return.
DollarBrace <~~ push(mkParamExp(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "${" :whitespace() PeBody "}" ;
PeBody      = PeLen | PeInd | PePatForm | PeCaseForm | PeReplForm | PeOpForm | PeSubstrForm | PePlain ;
PeLen       <~~ push({pe: "len"}) ~~> = "#" PeTarget ;
PeInd       <~~ push({pe: "ind"}) ~~> = "!" PeTarget ;
PeOpForm    = PeTarget PeOp [ BraceWord ] ;
PePlain     = PeTarget ;

// NAME or NAME[SUBSCRIPT] - the subscript is a word, read as an arithmetic index for an
// indexed array and as a literal key for an associative one. "@" and "*" mean the whole array.
PeTarget    = PeIdxRef | PeName ;
PeIdxRef    <~~ push({hasIdx: 1}) ~~> = PeName "[" IdxWord "]" ;
IdxWord     <~~ push(mkWord(takeAll())) ~~> = { IdxSeg } ;
IdxSeg      = SqSeg | DqSeg | ExpSeg | EscSeg | IdxBare ;
IdxBare     <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = !@+"]}\"'\\$`" ;
PeName      <~~ push({pname: up.in}) ~~> = PeNameTok ;
PeNameTok   = PeIdent | PeDigits | "@" | "*" | "#" | "?" | "!" | "$" | "-" ;
PeIdent     = ( "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "_" ) { "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "0"..."9" | "_" } ;
PeDigits    = "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" } ;
// Longest first: ":-" must be tried before "-" (see the ordered-choice rule).
PeOp        <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = ":-" | ":=" | ":?" | ":+" | "-" | "=" | "?" | "+" ;
// ${v#pat} ${v##pat} ${v%pat} ${v%%pat} : strip a matching prefix / suffix (# from the
// front, % from the back; doubled = longest match).
PePatForm   = PeTarget PePatOp PePatArg ;
PePatOp     <~~ push({pe: up.in}) ~~> = "##" | "#" | "%%" | "%" ;
PePatArg    <~~ push(mkWord(takeAll())) ~~> = { BraceSeg } ;

// ${v^} ${v^^} ${v,} ${v,,} : case conversion of the first / of every character.
PeCaseForm  = PeTarget PeCaseOp [ PePatArg ] ;
PeCaseOp    <~~ push({pe: up.in}) ~~> = "^^" | "^" | ",," | "," ;

// ${v/pat/rep} first match, ${v//pat/rep} every match, ${v/#pat/rep} anchored at the
// start, ${v/%pat/rep} anchored at the end. The replacement may be omitted (delete).
PeReplForm  = PeTarget PeReplOp [ PeReplAnchor ] ReplPat [ ReplTail ] ;
PeReplOp    <~~ push({pe: up.in}) ~~> = "//" | "/" ;
PeReplAnchor <~~ push({anchor: up.in}) ~~> = "#" | "%" ;
ReplPat     <~~ push(mkWord(takeAll())) ~~> = { ReplSeg } ;
ReplSeg     = SqSeg | DqSeg | ExpSeg | EscSeg | ReplBare ;
ReplBare    <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = !@+"}/\"'\\$`" ;
ReplTail    = "/" PePatArg ;

// ${v:off} and ${v:off:len} : the offsets are arithmetic, so whitespace matters again -
// which is also what tells ${v: -1} (a substring) from ${v:-default}.
PeSubstrForm <~~ push({pe: "substr"}) ~~> = PeTarget ":" :whitespace(Whitespace) ArList [ PeSubLen ] ;
PeSubLen    = ":" ArList ;

// A word inside ${...}: anything up to the closing brace, expansions included.
BraceWord   <~~ push(mkWord(takeAll())) ~~> = BraceSeg { BraceSeg } ;
BraceSeg    = SqSeg | DqSeg | ExpSeg | EscSeg | BraceBare ;
BraceBare   <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = !@+"}\"'\\$`" ;
DollarName  = "$" VarName <~~ push(mkVarRef(up.in)) ~~> ;
DollarDigit = "$" ArgDigit <~~ push(mkVarRef(up.in)) ~~> ;
DollarSpecial = DollarHash | DollarQ | DollarStar | DollarAt | DollarPid | DollarBang | DollarDash ;
DollarHash  <~~ push(mkVarRef("#")) ~~> = "$#" ;
DollarQ     <~~ push(mkVarRef("?")) ~~> = "$?" ;
DollarStar  <~~ push(mkVarRef("*")) ~~> = "$*" ;
DollarAt    <~~ push(mkVarRef("@")) ~~> = "$@" ;
DollarPid   <~~ push(mkVarRef("$")) ~~> = "$$" ;
DollarBang  <~~ push(mkVarRef("!")) ~~> = "$!" ;
DollarDash  <~~ push(mkVarRef("-")) ~~> = "$-" ;
DollarLit   <~~ push(makeConst("$")) ~~> = "$" ;

VarName     = ( "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "_" ) :whitespace() { "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "0"..."9" | "_" } ;
ArgDigit    = "0"..."9" ;


// --- $(( )) arithmetic: the full bash operator set ---------------------------------
// Precedence, loosest first: , then assignment, ?:, ||, &&, |, ^, &, == !=, comparison,
// shifts, + -, * / %, ** (right associative), unary, primary.

ArList      <~~ push(foldComma(takeAll())) ~~> = ArAssign { "," ArAssign } ;
ArAssign    = ArAssignForm | ArCond ;
ArAssignForm <~~ push(mkArAssign(takeAll())) ~~> = ArLhs ArAsgOp ArAssign ;
ArLhs       <~~ push({lhs: up.in}) ~~> = ArName ;
ArAsgOp     <~~ push({aop: up.in}) ~~>
            = "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/=" | "%=" | "<<=" | ">>=" | "&=" | "^=" | "|=" | ArPlainAsg ;
ArPlainAsg  = "=" NotEq ;

ArCond      <~~ push(mkArCond(takeAll())) ~~> = ArLor [ "?" ArAssign ":" ArAssign ] ;
ArLor       <~~ push(foldBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ArLand { ArLorOp ArLand } ;
ArLorOp     <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = "||" ;
ArLand      <~~ push(foldBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ArBor { ArLandOp ArBor } ;
ArLandOp    <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = "&&" ;
ArBor       <~~ push(foldBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ArBxor { ArBorOp ArBxor } ;
ArBorOp     <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = "|" NotBar ;
ArBxor      <~~ push(foldBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ArBand { ArBxorOp ArBand } ;
ArBxorOp    <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = "^" NotEq ;
ArBand      <~~ push(foldBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ArEq { ArBandOp ArEq } ;
ArBandOp    <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = "&" NotAmp ;
ArEq        <~~ push(foldBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ArCmp { ArEqOp ArCmp } ;
ArEqOp      <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = "==" | "!=" ;
ArCmp       <~~ push(foldBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ArShift { ArCmpOp ArShift } ;
ArCmpOp     <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = "<=" | ">=" | "<" | ">" ;
ArShift     <~~ push(foldBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ArAdd { ArShiftOp ArAdd } ;
ArShiftOp   <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = "<<" | ">>" ;
ArAdd       <~~ push(foldBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ArMul { ArAddOp ArMul } ;
ArAddOp     <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = "+" NotPlus | "-" NotMinus ;
ArMul       <~~ push(foldBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ArPow { ArMulOp ArPow } ;
ArMulOp     <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = "*" NotStar | "/" | "%" ;
ArPow       <~~ push(foldPow(takeAll())) ~~> = ArUnary [ ArPowOp ArPow ] ;
ArPowOp     <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = "**" ;

ArUnary     = ArPreInc | ArNeg | ArPos | ArNot | ArBnot | ArPrimary ;
ArPreInc    <~~ push(mkIncDec(takeAll())) ~~> = ArIncOp ArLhs ;
ArIncOp     <~~ push({iop: up.in, pre: 1}) ~~> = "++" | "--" ;
ArNeg       <~~ push(mkUn("-", pop())) ~~> = "-" ArUnary ;
ArPos       <~~ push(mkUn("+", pop())) ~~> = "+" ArUnary ;
ArNot       <~~ push(mkUn("!", pop())) ~~> = "!" NotEq ArUnary ;
ArBnot      <~~ push(mkUn("~", pop())) ~~> = "~" ArUnary ;

// A $-prefixed operand (Bash expands it before evaluating): $name, $1..$9, $# and $?.
ArPrimary   = ArGroup | ArNum | ArPostInc | ArCmdSub | ArDollarDigit | ArDollarHash | ArDollarQ | ArDollarVar | ArDollarBrace | ArVar ;
ArGroup     = "(" ArList ")" ;
ArPostInc   <~~ push(mkIncDec(takeAll())) ~~> = ArLhs ArPostOp ;
ArPostOp    <~~ push({iop: up.in, pre: 0}) ~~> = "++" | "--" ;
ArNum       <~~ push(mkNum(up.in)) ~~> = ArNumTok :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
ArNumTok    = ArHex | ArBase | ArDec ;
ArHex       = ArHexPfx :whitespace() ArHexDig { ArHexDig } ;
ArHexPfx    = "0x" | "0X" ;
ArHexDig    = "0"..."9" | "a"..."f" | "A"..."F" ;
ArBase      = ArDec :whitespace() "#" ArAlnum { ArAlnum } ;
ArAlnum     = "0"..."9" | "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "@" | "_" ;
ArDec       = "0"..."9" :whitespace() { "0"..."9" } ;
ArVar       <~~ push(mkArVar(up.in)) ~~> = ArName ;
ArDollarVar = "$" ArName <~~ push(mkArVar(up.in)) ~~> ;
ArDollarBrace <~~ push(mkArOfStr(pop())) ~~> = DollarBrace :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
ArDollarDigit = "$" ArgDigit <~~ push(mkArVar(up.in)) ~~> :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
ArDollarHash <~~ push(mkArVar("#")) ~~> = "$#" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
ArDollarQ   <~~ push(mkArVar("?")) ~~> = "$?" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
ArName      = ( "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "_" ) :whitespace() { "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "0"..."9" | "_" } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// $( LIST ) inside $(( )) : the captured output is read as an integer.
ArCmdSub    <~~ push(mkArCmdSub(pop())) ~~> = CmdSub :whitespace(Whitespace) ;


// --- [[ ... ]] conditional expressions ----------------------------------------------

CondOr      <~~ push(foldCondOr(takeAll())) ~~> = CondAnd { "||" CondAnd } ;
CondAnd     <~~ push(foldCondAnd(takeAll())) ~~> = CondNot { "&&" CondNot } ;
CondNot     = CondNeg | CondPrim ;
CondNeg     <~~ push(mkCondNot(pop())) ~~> = "!" CondNot ;
CondPrim    = CondParen | CondUn | CondMatch | CondBin | CondSingle ;

// [[ STR =~ ERE ]] : the right hand side is a REGEX, so ( ) | + ? { } are ordinary
// characters there and need a word production of their own. Quoted parts stay literal and
// $ expansions still happen, exactly as in bash.
CondMatch   <~~ push(mkCondMatch(takeAll())) ~~> = Word CondMatchOp RegexWord ;
CondMatchOp <~~ push({cop: up.in}) ~~> = "=~" ;
RegexWord   <~~ push(mkWord(takeAll())) ~~> = RegexSeg :whitespace() { RegexSeg } ;
RegexSeg    = SqSeg | DqSeg | ExpSeg | EscSeg | RegexBare ;
RegexBare   <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = !@+" \t\n\r;&<>$'\"\\`" ;
CondParen   = "(" CondOr ")" ;
CondUn      <~~ push(mkCondUn(takeAll())) ~~> = CondUnOp Word ;
CondUnOp    <~~ push({cop: up.in}) ~~> = CondUnTok KwEnd ;
CondUnTok   = "-z" | "-n" | "-v" | "-e" | "-f" | "-d" | "-s" | "-r" | "-w" | "-x" | "-h" | "-L" | "-b" | "-c" | "-p" | "-S" | "-t" | "-o" ;
// The right hand side of == / != inside [[ ]] is a PATTERN, and a quoted part of it is
// literal (`[[ $s == "h*" ]]` does not glob), so quoted segments are glob-escaped here.
CondBin     <~~ push(mkCondBin(takeAll())) ~~> = Word CondBinOp PatWord ;
PatWord     <~~ push(mkWord(takeAll())) ~~> = PatSeg :whitespace() { PatSeg } ;
PatSeg      = PatSq | PatDq | PatAnsi | ExpSeg | PatEsc | BareSeg ;
PatSq       = "'" :whitespace() { !@+"'" } <~~ push(makeConst(globEscape(up.in))) ~~> "'" ;
PatDq       <~~ push(mkQuotedPat(mkDqWord(takeAll()))) ~~>
            = '"' :whitespace() { ExpSeg | DqCont | DqEsc | DqLit } '"' ;
PatAnsi     = "$'" :whitespace() PatAnsiBody "'" ;
PatAnsiBody <~~ push(makeConst(globEscape(ansiC(up.in)))) ~~> = { AnsiEsc | AnsiLit } ;
PatEsc      <~~ push(makeConst(globEscape(escChar(up.in)))) ~~> = "\\" AnyCh ;
CondBinOp   <~~ push({cop: up.in}) ~~> = CondBinTok ;
CondBinTok  = "==" | "!=" | "-eq" | "-ne" | "-lt" | "-le" | "-gt" | "-ge" | "-nt" | "-ot" | "-ef" | "=" | "<" | ">" ;
CondSingle  <~~ push(mkCondSingle(pop())) ~~> = Word ;


// --- keywords, keyword boundary and the reserved-word guard ------------------------

KwIf        = "if" KwEnd ;
KwThen      = "then" KwEnd ;
KwElif      = "elif" KwEnd ;
KwElse      = "else" KwEnd ;
KwFi        = "fi" KwEnd ;
KwFor       = "for" KwEnd ;
KwWhile     = "while" KwEnd ;
KwDo        = "do" KwEnd ;
KwDone      = "done" KwEnd ;
KwIn        = "in" KwEnd ;
KwCase      = "case" KwEnd ;
KwUntil     = "until" KwEnd ;
KwFunction  = "function" KwEnd ;
KwEsac      = "esac" KwEnd ;

// Fails when the next thing (after any run of blanks and newlines) is a clause terminator,
// so a case body's separator run stops in front of ";;" / ";&" / ";;&".
// "|" that is not the front of "||", and "<" that is not the front of "<<" - both anchored
// to the very next byte, which a !"literal" lookahead cannot do (it skips whitespace first).
NotBar = :script(~~
    (function() {
        if (c.peek(0) === 124) { return abnf.newToken("\x01bar", 0) }
    })()
~~) ;
NotLt = :script(~~
    (function() {
        if (c.peek(0) === 60) { return abnf.newToken("\x01lt", 0) }
    })()
~~) ;

// A tilde only expands when the word ends there or continues with "/".
TildeEnd = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var cc = c.peek(0)
        if (cc == -1 || cc == 47) { return undefined }
        if (cc == 32 || cc == 9 || cc == 10 || cc == 13) { return undefined }
        if (cc == 59 || cc == 38 || cc == 124 || cc == 41 || cc == 62 || cc == 60) { return undefined }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01tilde", 0)
    })()
~~) ;

HdScan = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var no = abnf.newToken("\x01noheredoc", 0)
        // Whitespace is not skipped before a script, so step over blanks exactly like the
        // token match that follows will.
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc === 32 || cc === 9) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i) }
        var start = i
        if (c.peek(i) !== 60 || c.peek(i + 1) !== 60) { return no }
        i = i + 2
        if (c.peek(i) === 60) { return no }           // <<< is a here-STRING
        var dashed = false
        if (c.peek(i) === 45) { dashed = true; i = i + 1 }
        while (c.peek(i) === 32 || c.peek(i) === 9) { i = i + 1 }
        var quote = 0
        var q = c.peek(i)
        if (q === 39 || q === 34) { quote = q; i = i + 1 }
        var id = ""
        var ch = c.peek(i)
        while ((ch >= 48 && ch <= 57) || (ch >= 65 && ch <= 90) || (ch >= 97 && ch <= 122) || ch === 95) {
            id = id + String.fromCharCode(ch)
            i = i + 1
            ch = c.peek(i)
        }
        if (id === "") { return no }
        if (quote !== 0) {
            if (c.peek(i) !== quote) { return no }
            i = i + 1
        }
        // Nothing but blanks may follow the marker on its own line.
        var j = i
        var dd = c.peek(j)
        while (dd === 32 || dd === 9 || dd === 13) { j = j + 1; dd = c.peek(j) }
        if (dd !== 10) { return no }
        j = j + 1
        // Walk whole lines until one is exactly the marker (leading tabs ignored for <<-),
        // and stop just BEFORE that line's newline.
        var end = -1
        var guard = 0
        while (guard < 1000000) {
            guard = guard + 1
            var k = j
            var b = c.peek(k)
            if (b === -1) { end = k; guard = 1000000; continue }
            if (dashed) { while (b === 9) { k = k + 1; b = c.peek(k) } }
            var mm = 0
            while (mm < id.length && c.peek(k + mm) === id.charCodeAt(mm)) { mm = mm + 1 }
            var after = c.peek(k + mm)
            if (mm === id.length && (after === 10 || after === 13 || after === -1)) {
                end = k + mm
                guard = 1000000
                continue
            }
            while (b !== 10 && b !== -1) { k = k + 1; b = c.peek(k) }
            if (b === -1) { return no }
            j = k + 1
        }
        if (end < 0) { return no }
        // Rebuild the exact source slice as a token. c.peek answers BYTES, so the UTF-8 has
        // to be decoded here or a non-ASCII body would never match.
        var out = ""
        var p = start
        while (p < end) {
            var b0 = c.peek(p)
            if (b0 < 128) { out = out + String.fromCharCode(b0); p = p + 1; continue }
            if (b0 >= 240) {
                var cp4 = ((b0 - 240) * 262144) + ((c.peek(p + 1) - 128) * 4096) + ((c.peek(p + 2) - 128) * 64) + (c.peek(p + 3) - 128)
                var off = cp4 - 65536
                out = out + String.fromCharCode(55296 + ((off - (off % 1024)) / 1024)) + String.fromCharCode(56320 + (off % 1024))
                p = p + 4
                continue
            }
            if (b0 >= 224) {
                out = out + String.fromCharCode(((b0 - 224) * 4096) + ((c.peek(p + 1) - 128) * 64) + (c.peek(p + 2) - 128))
                p = p + 3
                continue
            }
            if (b0 >= 192) {
                out = out + String.fromCharCode(((b0 - 192) * 64) + (c.peek(p + 1) - 128))
                p = p + 2
                continue
            }
            return no
        }
        return abnf.newToken(out, 0)
    })()
~~) ;

NotEq    = :script(~~ (function() { if (c.peek(0) === 61) { return abnf.newToken("\x01eq", 0) } })() ~~) ;
NotAmp   = :script(~~ (function() { if (c.peek(0) === 38) { return abnf.newToken("\x01amp", 0) } })() ~~) ;
NotStar  = :script(~~ (function() { if (c.peek(0) === 42) { return abnf.newToken("\x01star", 0) } })() ~~) ;
NotPlus  = :script(~~ (function() { if (c.peek(0) === 43 || c.peek(0) === 61) { return abnf.newToken("\x01plus", 0) } })() ~~) ;
NotMinus = :script(~~ (function() { if (c.peek(0) === 45 || c.peek(0) === 61) { return abnf.newToken("\x01minus", 0) } })() ~~) ;

NotCaseTerm = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc == 32 || cc == 9 || cc == 10 || cc == 13) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i) }
        if (cc == 59) {
            var nx = c.peek(i + 1)
            if (nx == 59 || nx == 38) { return abnf.newToken("\x01case-term", 0) }
        }
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// A keyword only counts when the next char is a word terminator (whitespace, a separator or
// a metacharacter), so 'done1', 'ifx' and 'in_it' stay ordinary words. Returns the impossible
// token to FAIL (word continues), undefined to succeed (real boundary).
KwEnd = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var cc = c.peek(0)
        if (cc == -1) { return undefined }
        if (cc == 32 || cc == 9 || cc == 10 || cc == 13) { return undefined }
        if (cc == 59 || cc == 38 || cc == 124) { return undefined }
        if (cc == 40 || cc == 41 || cc == 60 || cc == 62 || cc == 35) { return undefined }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01kw-boundary", 0)
    })()
~~) ;

// Succeeds (consumes nothing) unless the upcoming word is a reserved keyword with a boundary,
// in which case it fails so the SimpleCommand does not swallow the keyword.
NotReserved = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc == 32 || cc == 9) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i) }
        // "}" standing alone is the reserved word that closes a brace group, never a command
        // (a bare "{" or "}" IS an ordinary word character elsewhere, for {single} and a}b).
        if (cc == 125) {
            var nb = c.peek(i + 1)
            if (nb == -1 || nb == 32 || nb == 9 || nb == 10 || nb == 13 || nb == 59 || nb == 38 || nb == 124 || nb == 41) {
                return abnf.newToken("\x01close-brace", 0)
            }
        }
        var w = ""
        cc = c.peek(i)
        while ((cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90)) { w = w + String.fromCharCode(cc); i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i) }
        var boundary = (cc == -1 || cc == 32 || cc == 9 || cc == 10 || cc == 13 || cc == 59 || cc == 38 || cc == 124 || cc == 40 || cc == 41 || cc == 60 || cc == 62 || cc == 35)
        var kw = (w == "if" || w == "then" || w == "elif" || w == "else" || w == "fi" || w == "for" || w == "while" || w == "do" || w == "done" || w == "in" || w == "case" || w == "esac" || w == "until" || w == "function")
        if (kw && boundary) { return abnf.newToken("\x01reserved-word", 0) }
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;


// --- whitespace: spaces, tabs, CR and # line comments; NEVER a newline (that is structure).

Whitespace  = { @+" \t\r" | Comment } ;
Comment     = "#" :whitespace() { NotNL } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
NotNL       = "\x00"..."\x09" | "\x0b"..."\U0010ffff" ;


// ---

:startScript(~~

    // ===================================================================================
    //  Stack / string helpers (self contained, no shared core).
    //  NOTE: the local stack preserves plain objects but strips expando properties off
    //  pushed FUNCTIONS, so a string-producing thunk is carried as a plain object
    //  {fn: function(b) -> {b, v}, c: constant-string-or-undefined}. Statement and
    //  arithmetic thunks, which need no attached data, stay bare functions.
    // ===================================================================================

    function takeAll() {
        var items = []
        var v = anytype
        while ((v = pop()) != null) items.unshift(v)
        return items
    }
    function hasOwn(o, name) { return Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(o, name) }
    function fail(msg) { println("bash-to-llvm-ir error: " + msg); exit(2) }

    // $'...' body -> the decoded string (bash's ANSI-C escapes).
    function ansiC(t) {
        var out = ""
        var i = 0
        while (i < t.length) {
            var ch = t.charAt(i)
            if (ch != "\\" || i + 1 >= t.length) { out = out + ch; i = i + 1 }
            else {
                var e = t.charAt(i + 1)
                i = i + 2
                if (e == "n") { out = out + "\n" }
                else if (e == "t") { out = out + "\t" }
                else if (e == "r") { out = out + "\r" }
                else if (e == "a") { out = out + String.fromCharCode(7) }
                else if (e == "b") { out = out + String.fromCharCode(8) }
                else if (e == "e" || e == "E") { out = out + String.fromCharCode(27) }
                else if (e == "f") { out = out + String.fromCharCode(12) }
                else if (e == "v") { out = out + String.fromCharCode(11) }
                else if (e == "0") { out = out + String.fromCharCode(0) }
                else if (e == "\\") { out = out + "\\" }
                else if (e == "'") { out = out + "'" }
                else if (e == "\"") { out = out + "\"" }
                else if (e == "x") {
                    var v = 0, n = 0
                    while (n < 2 && i < t.length && hexVal(t.charCodeAt(i)) >= 0) { v = v * 16 + hexVal(t.charCodeAt(i)); i = i + 1; n = n + 1 }
                    out = out + String.fromCharCode(v)
                }
                else { out = out + "\\" + e }
            }
        }
        return out
    }
    function hexVal(cc) {
        if (cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) { return cc - 48 }
        if (cc >= 97 && cc <= 102) { return cc - 87 }
        if (cc >= 65 && cc <= 70) { return cc - 55 }
        return -1
    }

    function escChar(t) { return t.slice(1) }
    function dqEsc(t) {
        var c = t.slice(1)
        if (c == "$" || c == "\"" || c == "\\" || c == "`") { return c }
        return t
    }
    function parseDec(s) {
        var neg = false, i = 0
        if (s.charAt(0) == "-") { neg = true; i = 1 } else if (s.charAt(0) == "+") { i = 1 }
        var v = 0
        while (i < s.length) { v = (v * 10 + (s.charCodeAt(i) - 48)) | 0; i = i + 1 }
        return neg ? ((0 - v) | 0) : v
    }
    function utf8Len(s) {
        var n = 0, i = 0
        while (i < s.length) {
            var c = s.charCodeAt(i)
            if (c < 0x80) { n += 1 }
            else if (c < 0x800) { n += 2 }
            else if (c >= 0xd800 && c <= 0xdbff) { n += 4; i += 1 }
            else { n += 3 }
            i += 1
        }
        return n
    }

    // ===================================================================================
    //  LLVM types, module, externs and the runtime globals.
    // ===================================================================================

    var i8 = llvm.types.I8
    var i32 = llvm.types.I32
    var i8ptr = llvm.types.I8Ptr
    var zero = llvm.constant.NewInt(i32, 0)
    var one = llvm.constant.NewInt(i32, 1)
    var ten = llvm.constant.NewInt(i32, 10)
    var zero8 = llvm.constant.NewInt(i8, 0)
    var nullStr = llvm.constant.NewNull(i8ptr) // the empty string: address 0 is the NUL byte

    var m = llvm.ir.NewModule()
    var extPutchar = m.NewFunc("putchar", i32, llvm.ir.NewParam("", i32))

    // The string heap: one big byte arena plus a bump offset. rt_bump hands out slices.
    var ARENA = 1 << 22
    var arenaTy = llvm.types.NewArray(ARENA, i8)
    var gArena = m.NewGlobalDef("arena", llvm.constant.NewZeroInitializer(arenaTy))
    var gApos = m.NewGlobalDef("arena_pos", zero)

    var gStatus = m.NewGlobalDef("last_status", zero) // $?
    var gExited = m.NewGlobalDef("exited", zero)       // set by `exit`
    var gExitCode = m.NewGlobalDef("exit_code", zero)

    // A real "" at a NONZERO address. The IR interpreter traps loads at address 0, so an
    // empty / unset value must be a genuine pointer to a NUL byte, never a null pointer.
    var gEmpty = m.NewGlobalDef("empty", llvm.constant.NewCharArrayFromString("\x00"))
    var emptyTy = llvm.types.NewArray(1, i8)
    function emptyPtr(b) { return b.NewGetElementPtr(emptyTy, gEmpty, zero, zero) }

